other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/FrozenPandaz","id":8104246,"login":"FrozenPandaz","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMDQyNDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/6e2b778e4eca78fa84f263830ba63807d4238e9d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T14:59:20Z","email":"jasonjean1993@gmail.com","name":"Jason Jean"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T14:59:20Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(repo): run eslint, eslint-plugin and oxlint unit tests with vitest (#37174)\n\nStacked on #37173.\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`@nx/eslint` (`packages/eslint`), `@nx/oxlint` (`packages/oxlint`) and\n`@nx/eslint-plugin` (`packages/eslint-plugin`) run their unit tests with\nJest.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nAll three packages run their unit tests with Vitest, inferred by\n`@nx/vitest` from a `vitest.config.mts` that reuses the shared\n`tools/vitest/setup.mts` and `tools/vitest/nx-source-resolver.mts`. Each\n`jest.config.cts` is deleted. The shared setup is unchanged.\n\n| Package | Tests (jest → vitest) | `nx test` wall (jest → vitest) |\nRunner time (jest → vitest) |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------ |\n------------------------------ | --------------------------- |\n| `@nx/eslint` | 607 (1 skipped) → 607 (1 skipped) | 61.4s → 7.0s |\n60.1s → 5.6s |\n| `@nx/oxlint` | 88 → 88 | 4.7s → 3.4s | 3.4s → 2.4s |\n| `@nx/eslint-plugin` | 211 → 211 | 4.2s → 2.9s | 3.1s → 1.9s |\n\nTiming method: medians of 3 alternating rounds under `caffeinate -i`.\nEach round ran jest on a detached checkout of the base branch, then\nvitest on this branch. The daemon was on and warmed with two `nx show\nprojects` calls after each checkout. Each run was `nx test <p>\n--skip-nx-cache --excludeTaskDependencies`.\n\nRaw (wall / runner):\n\n- eslint jest: 61.35/60.11, 64.60/63.41, 60.35/59.19\n- eslint vitest: 7.60/6.64, 6.95/5.62, 6.28/5.27\n- oxlint jest: 4.69/3.45, 4.68/3.42, 4.38/3.36\n- oxlint vitest: 3.59/2.61, 3.28/2.33, 3.35/2.36\n\n### Hand fixes\n\n- **memfs through the CJS channel** (eslint-plugin `runtime-lint-utils`,\n`enforce-module-boundaries`, `dependency-checks` specs): import-path and\ntsconfig resolution load typescript with `require`, which reads through\nthe CJS `fs` it grabbed at load, so the spec''s `vi.mock(''fs'')` memfs\nnever reached it. The specs register memfs as `fs` with `mockCjsModule`\nand evict typescript from `require.cache` so it reloads onto memfs.\n- **Async mock factories** (`lint.impl.spec.ts`, eslint\n`plugin.spec.ts`): the codemod left a `requireActual` inside a class\nstatic field and inside a sync function in a mock factory. Both now load\nthe actual module once with `await vi.importActual` at the top level or\nin an async factory.\n- **`loadESLint` stub** (`eslint-utils.spec.ts`): the spec replaced\n`loadESLint` on `require(''eslint'')`. That is the real CJS module, not\nthe `vi.mock`ed one the source imports. It now stubs the mocked ESM\nbinding with `vi.mocked(loadESLint)`.\n- **Live `workspaceRoot`** (eslint and oxlint `plugin.spec.ts`): the\nshared setup''s `@nx/devkit` mock spreads the module, so `workspaceRoot`\nis copied once. When `TempFs` later moves it,\n`getRootTsConfigFileName()` from `@nx/js` still reads the old root. Each\nspec now mocks `@nx/devkit` with a getter that returns the live value.\nThis fix stays in the specs, so the shared setup is unchanged.\n\n`@nx/oxlint` is ESM-only (`\"type\": \"module\"`) and imports `./x.js`\nspecifiers that point at `.ts` files. Vite resolves these without extra\nconfig, so its `vitest.config.mts` matches the other packages.\n\n### Snapshot parity\n\n- eslint-plugin has no `.snap` files and no inline snapshot changed. The\n3 eslint `.snap` files were regenerated from pristine copies. There are\n40 keys and 0 value diffs after normalizing the `>` separator.\n- 6 inline `toThrowErrorMatchingInlineSnapshot` updates are format-only:\n`\"msg\"` became `[Error: msg]`.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `nx run-many -t test,build,lint,oxlint,build-base,copy-assets -p\neslint @nx/oxlint devkit --skip-nx-cache`: green. This covers every\ntarget both projects list.\n- eslint-plugin: `nx run-many -t test,build,lint,oxlint -p eslint-plugin\njest --skip-nx-cache`: green (`jest` stays on Jest and is the canary),\nplus two repeat runs.\n- `nx sync:check`: green.\n- Two repeat `nx test` runs of both packages: green, with the same\ncounts.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nN/A\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/Run-rspack-and-rsbuild-unit-tests-with-vitest-4a161f55\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->","tree":{"sha":"d4e80128424d5e4d342f129a5123dd6d3364869b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/trees/d4e80128424d5e4d342f129a5123dd6d3364869b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/commits/6e2b778e4eca78fa84f263830ba63807d4238e9d","verification":{"payload":"tree d4e80128424d5e4d342f129a5123dd6d3364869b\nparent d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94\nauthor Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com> 1790434760 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790434760 -0400\n\nchore(repo): run eslint, eslint-plugin and oxlint unit tests with vitest (#37174)\n\nStacked on #37173.\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`@nx/eslint` (`packages/eslint`), `@nx/oxlint` (`packages/oxlint`) and\n`@nx/eslint-plugin` (`packages/eslint-plugin`) run their unit tests with\nJest.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nAll three packages run their unit tests with Vitest, inferred by\n`@nx/vitest` from a `vitest.config.mts` that reuses the shared\n`tools/vitest/setup.mts` and `tools/vitest/nx-source-resolver.mts`. Each\n`jest.config.cts` is deleted. The shared setup is unchanged.\n\n| Package | Tests (jest → vitest) | `nx test` wall (jest → vitest) |\nRunner time (jest → vitest) |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------ |\n------------------------------ | --------------------------- |\n| `@nx/eslint` | 607 (1 skipped) → 607 (1 skipped) | 61.4s → 7.0s |\n60.1s → 5.6s |\n| `@nx/oxlint` | 88 → 88 | 4.7s → 3.4s | 3.4s → 2.4s |\n| `@nx/eslint-plugin` | 211 → 211 | 4.2s → 2.9s | 3.1s → 1.9s |\n\nTiming method: medians of 3 alternating rounds under `caffeinate -i`.\nEach round ran jest on a detached checkout of the base branch, then\nvitest on this branch. The daemon was on and warmed with two `nx show\nprojects` calls after each checkout. Each run was `nx test <p>\n--skip-nx-cache --excludeTaskDependencies`.\n\nRaw (wall / runner):\n\n- eslint jest: 61.35/60.11, 64.60/63.41, 60.35/59.19\n- eslint vitest: 7.60/6.64, 6.95/5.62, 6.28/5.27\n- oxlint jest: 4.69/3.45, 4.68/3.42, 4.38/3.36\n- oxlint vitest: 3.59/2.61, 3.28/2.33, 3.35/2.36\n\n### Hand fixes\n\n- **memfs through the CJS channel** (eslint-plugin `runtime-lint-utils`,\n`enforce-module-boundaries`, `dependency-checks` specs): import-path and\ntsconfig resolution load typescript with `require`, which reads through\nthe CJS `fs` it grabbed at load, so the spec''s `vi.mock(''fs'')` memfs\nnever reached it. The specs register memfs as `fs` with `mockCjsModule`\nand evict typescript from `require.cache` so it reloads onto memfs.\n- **Async mock factories** (`lint.impl.spec.ts`, eslint\n`plugin.spec.ts`): the codemod left a `requireActual` inside a class\nstatic field and inside a sync function in a mock factory. Both now load\nthe actual module once with `await vi.importActual` at the top level or\nin an async factory.\n- **`loadESLint` stub** (`eslint-utils.spec.ts`): the spec replaced\n`loadESLint` on `require(''eslint'')`. That is the real CJS module, not\nthe `vi.mock`ed one the source imports. It now stubs the mocked ESM\nbinding with `vi.mocked(loadESLint)`.\n- **Live `workspaceRoot`** (eslint and oxlint `plugin.spec.ts`): the\nshared setup''s `@nx/devkit` mock spreads the module, so `workspaceRoot`\nis copied once. When `TempFs` later moves it,\n`getRootTsConfigFileName()` from `@nx/js` still reads the old root. Each\nspec now mocks `@nx/devkit` with a getter that returns the live value.\nThis fix stays in the specs, so the shared setup is unchanged.\n\n`@nx/oxlint` is ESM-only (`\"type\": \"module\"`) and imports `./x.js`\nspecifiers that point at `.ts` files. Vite resolves these without extra\nconfig, so its `vitest.config.mts` matches the other packages.\n\n### Snapshot parity\n\n- eslint-plugin has no `.snap` files and no inline snapshot changed. The\n3 eslint `.snap` files were regenerated from pristine copies. There are\n40 keys and 0 value diffs after normalizing the `>` separator.\n- 6 inline `toThrowErrorMatchingInlineSnapshot` updates are format-only:\n`\"msg\"` became `[Error: msg]`.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `nx run-many -t test,build,lint,oxlint,build-base,copy-assets -p\neslint @nx/oxlint devkit --skip-nx-cache`: green. This covers every\ntarget both projects list.\n- eslint-plugin: `nx run-many -t test,build,lint,oxlint -p eslint-plugin\njest --skip-nx-cache`: green (`jest` stays on Jest and is the canary),\nplus two repeat runs.\n- `nx sync:check`: green.\n- Two repeat `nx test` runs of both packages: green, with the same\ncounts.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nN/A\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/Run-rspack-and-rsbuild-unit-tests-with-vitest-4a161f55\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqt93ICRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAoCAQAG9TTinu7h3F+uAS7/tH2vhj\nrRcO+K4SItbSdYVX7zpI4xmg5FBIint5zMV2uhdyRHWzVgCZfsNmPB1w07dhnhba\nRkB7LrX5qSZGYV6Dy/yJPaRU0xx9AuGcOxbljF+bkV+J3zumExOL9Jbj9AHX8wiW\n/bPpKmfWKMjn1nNNAUE84Wtp91Kc/Xhfmo20e2k8Bqucp09e3A7tUE7ydhWlpEhn\ndChS7ngL+dLB/05bU8s1QYSf+Rn4zRv2yVCVJI/Hq377sbUy0sKaop47ybtYQKvc\nw/sbRbWvlbBMxM/zeE7YjYk5eA0bhm3LaDSvDH12lZnPWZNhlnBkHJu2DY4YRTZZ\nkg5fb5m37h2f/P3MLQPMLnMpmZOlxTSzpryc4QcDbzvOoEVlE+1i0Am+X7ob4fja\nNmQGR+sBqAkt/ECAMXtDEZWUnPa3rnLBgbpsYY884VbqII8ppMUd9VpZI5Du3gBF\nWtbeIZ6hdKHgbM73VcH5++wQ3wAc1IEcu46/7MeIieVuPterMZGAPV7NKBt/WS2c\nLae2m3S9NFZ7Kkq2TV54GqPINCqNRy0ByfChhPNh0HCuq6hDXGz8Z0fnTCvlG6/z\nqU+JgW6SDMCkNzGIog6C1TodFmSEZ8sKXxdJIjqQ3WuRL4d/WCAganZQpQIPkIgr\nBYqcU7ghSv2Ww2GVKrOl\n=uYWA\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-26T14:59:21Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/6e2b778e4eca78fa84f263830ba63807d4238e9d","node_id":"C_kwDOBfbSFNoAKDZlMmI3NzhlNGVjYTc4ZmE4NGYyNjM4MzBiYTYzODA3ZDQyMzhlOWQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94","sha":"d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94"}],"sha":"6e2b778e4eca78fa84f263830ba63807d4238e9d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/6e2b778e4eca78fa84f263830ba63807d4238e9d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8104246?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/FrozenPandaz","id":8104246,"login":"FrozenPandaz","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMDQyNDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T05:21:22Z","email":"jasonjean1993@gmail.com","name":"Jason Jean"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T05:21:22Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(repo): run remix, next, expo and react-native unit tests with vitest (#37173)\n\nStacked on #37172.\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`@nx/remix`, `@nx/next`, `@nx/expo` and `@nx/react-native` run their\nunit tests with Jest through `jest.preset.js`.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nAll four packages run their unit tests with Vitest 4. The `@nx/vitest`\nplugin infers the target, and each one reuses the shared\n`tools/vitest/setup.mts` and `tools/vitest/nx-source-resolver.mts`. Each\npackage gets a `vitest.config.mts` copied from `packages/react`, a\n`test.inputs` block naming the shared setup files, and `vitest/globals`\ntypes in `tsconfig.spec.json`. Its `jest.config.cts` is deleted.\n\n`tools/vitest/setup.mts` is **unchanged**.\n\n| Package | Tests (jest → vitest) | `nx test` wall, median (jest →\nvitest) | Runner-reported, median (jest → vitest) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `remix` | 177 (1 skipped) → 177 (1 skipped) | 162.4s → 7.3s | 161.1s →\n6.2s |\n| `next` | 227 → 227 | 136.5s → 7.6s | 135.0s → 6.6s |\n| `expo` | 188 → 188 | 131.7s → 8.3s | 130.4s → 7.4s |\n| `react-native` | 138 → 138 | 78.4s → 5.5s | 77.0s → 4.6s |\n\nTiming method: 3 rounds under `caffeinate -i`. Each round ran jest on a\ndetached checkout of the base branch, then vitest on this branch, for\nall four packages. After each checkout, `nx show projects` ran twice to\nwarm the daemon. Each timing is one `NX_DAEMON=true pnpm nx test <p>\n--skip-nx-cache --excludeTaskDependencies` run, and the tables report\nthe median of the 3 rounds.\n\n### Hand fixes (beyond the `jest.*` → `vi.*` codemod)\n\n- **`next`, `expo` and `react-native` `convert-to-inferred` specs.** The\n`nx/src/devkit-internals` mock was a lazy `Proxy` that called sync\n`jest.requireActual`. It is now an async factory that uses\n`vi.importActual`, the same shape as the rspack spec. This change is\nfolded into each package''s codemod commit.\n- **`expo` migration specs.**\n`jest.mock(''expo/src/winter/ImportMetaRegistry'', …)` appears inside\nfixture strings in `update-22-2-0` and `update-23-1-0`. These strings\nwere reverted after the codemod.\n- **`remix` `create-watch-paths.spec.ts`.** The spec spied on\n`require(''@nx/devkit'')`, but the source imports it as ESM. It now spies\non `import * as devkit`.\n- **`remix` `library.impl.spec.ts`.** `xit` → `it.skip`, because Vitest\nhas no `xit`.\n- **`next` `plugins/plugin.spec.ts`.** The `mockNextConfig` helper\ncalled `jest.mock(path, …, { virtual: true })`. Vitest hoists that call,\nso `path` was undefined. Under jest it had been a no-op, because the\ncall was not hoisted and the path was relative to the spec, not the temp\nroot. `loadConfigFile` loads the config with `require`, so the helper\nnow uses `mockCjsModule` on the real temp path, and `resetCjsMocks()`\nruns in `afterEach`.\n- **`expo` `plugins/with-nx-metro.spec.ts`.** The spec loads\n`with-nx-metro` with `require`, so its `@nx/devkit` import is on the CJS\nchannel and `vi.doMock` never reached it. It now uses\n`mockCjsModule(''@nx/devkit'', { workspaceRoot })`, evicts the module from\n`require.cache`, and calls `resetCjsMocks()` in `afterEach`.\n- **`react-native` `upgrade-react-native-projects.spec.ts`.** The spec\nread `execSync` through `require(''child_process'')`, which returns the\nreal module. It now imports the mocked ESM binding.\n\n### Snapshot parity\n\n- **`remix`: 7 `.snap` files, 50 keys.** They were regenerated from\npristine copies with `vitest run -u`. The value diff after key\nnormalization shows 0 changed, 0 missing and 0 extra.\n`library.impl.spec.ts.snap` kept its obsolete jest-format keys after\n`-u`, because the file contains a skipped test. Those 4 stale keys were\nstripped by hand.\n- **`next`: 2 `.snap` files, 4 keys.** The value diff shows 0 changed, 0\nmissing and 0 extra.\n- **`expo` and `react-native`** have no `.snap` files, and their inline\nsnapshots did not change under `-u`.\n- **Inline snapshot changes** are format-only. Two `remix` inline error\nsnapshots changed from `\"msg\"` to `[Error: msg]`.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `nx run-many -t test,build,lint,oxlint -p remix next expo react-native\ndevkit --skip-nx-cache` passes. These are every runnable target the\nprojects list.\n- `nx sync:check` passes.\n- Two repeat `nx test` runs of all four packages pass with the same\ncounts.\n- The lint warnings in `devkit` and `next` (unused eslint-disable\ndirectives) predate this change.\n\n### Also in this PR: e2e base workspace templates on macOS\n\nThis PR''s macOS e2e run failed in `populate-e2e-base-workspace`: both\npnpm templates broke while being packed (`ENOENT: … lstat\n''…/proj/.git/objects/ea/209a…''`) and removed (`ENOTEMPTY`).\n`create-nx-workspace`''s initial commit can leave enough loose objects\nfor git to start gc/maintenance in the background, which deletes\n`.git/objects` files under the pack. The template builds now run with\n`gc.auto=0` and `maintenance.auto=false` (via `GIT_CONFIG_*`, appended\nto any already set). Locally, a 7,000-file commit had all 7,002 loose\nobjects deleted by background maintenance within seconds by default, and\nnone with these settings. (Previously opened separately as #37197.)\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nN/A\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/Run-rspack-and-rsbuild-unit-tests-with-vitest-4a161f55\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nx-cloud[bot] <71083854+nx-cloud[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"00166c1f7144fd6c4d4a0732860903aa0b42555b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/trees/00166c1f7144fd6c4d4a0732860903aa0b42555b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/commits/d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94","verification":{"payload":"tree 00166c1f7144fd6c4d4a0732860903aa0b42555b\nparent 8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842\nauthor Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com> 1790400082 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790400082 -0400\n\nchore(repo): run remix, next, expo and react-native unit tests with vitest (#37173)\n\nStacked on #37172.\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`@nx/remix`, `@nx/next`, `@nx/expo` and `@nx/react-native` run their\nunit tests with Jest through `jest.preset.js`.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nAll four packages run their unit tests with Vitest 4. The `@nx/vitest`\nplugin infers the target, and each one reuses the shared\n`tools/vitest/setup.mts` and `tools/vitest/nx-source-resolver.mts`. Each\npackage gets a `vitest.config.mts` copied from `packages/react`, a\n`test.inputs` block naming the shared setup files, and `vitest/globals`\ntypes in `tsconfig.spec.json`. Its `jest.config.cts` is deleted.\n\n`tools/vitest/setup.mts` is **unchanged**.\n\n| Package | Tests (jest → vitest) | `nx test` wall, median (jest →\nvitest) | Runner-reported, median (jest → vitest) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `remix` | 177 (1 skipped) → 177 (1 skipped) | 162.4s → 7.3s | 161.1s →\n6.2s |\n| `next` | 227 → 227 | 136.5s → 7.6s | 135.0s → 6.6s |\n| `expo` | 188 → 188 | 131.7s → 8.3s | 130.4s → 7.4s |\n| `react-native` | 138 → 138 | 78.4s → 5.5s | 77.0s → 4.6s |\n\nTiming method: 3 rounds under `caffeinate -i`. Each round ran jest on a\ndetached checkout of the base branch, then vitest on this branch, for\nall four packages. After each checkout, `nx show projects` ran twice to\nwarm the daemon. Each timing is one `NX_DAEMON=true pnpm nx test <p>\n--skip-nx-cache --excludeTaskDependencies` run, and the tables report\nthe median of the 3 rounds.\n\n### Hand fixes (beyond the `jest.*` → `vi.*` codemod)\n\n- **`next`, `expo` and `react-native` `convert-to-inferred` specs.** The\n`nx/src/devkit-internals` mock was a lazy `Proxy` that called sync\n`jest.requireActual`. It is now an async factory that uses\n`vi.importActual`, the same shape as the rspack spec. This change is\nfolded into each package''s codemod commit.\n- **`expo` migration specs.**\n`jest.mock(''expo/src/winter/ImportMetaRegistry'', …)` appears inside\nfixture strings in `update-22-2-0` and `update-23-1-0`. These strings\nwere reverted after the codemod.\n- **`remix` `create-watch-paths.spec.ts`.** The spec spied on\n`require(''@nx/devkit'')`, but the source imports it as ESM. It now spies\non `import * as devkit`.\n- **`remix` `library.impl.spec.ts`.** `xit` → `it.skip`, because Vitest\nhas no `xit`.\n- **`next` `plugins/plugin.spec.ts`.** The `mockNextConfig` helper\ncalled `jest.mock(path, …, { virtual: true })`. Vitest hoists that call,\nso `path` was undefined. Under jest it had been a no-op, because the\ncall was not hoisted and the path was relative to the spec, not the temp\nroot. `loadConfigFile` loads the config with `require`, so the helper\nnow uses `mockCjsModule` on the real temp path, and `resetCjsMocks()`\nruns in `afterEach`.\n- **`expo` `plugins/with-nx-metro.spec.ts`.** The spec loads\n`with-nx-metro` with `require`, so its `@nx/devkit` import is on the CJS\nchannel and `vi.doMock` never reached it. It now uses\n`mockCjsModule(''@nx/devkit'', { workspaceRoot })`, evicts the module from\n`require.cache`, and calls `resetCjsMocks()` in `afterEach`.\n- **`react-native` `upgrade-react-native-projects.spec.ts`.** The spec\nread `execSync` through `require(''child_process'')`, which returns the\nreal module. It now imports the mocked ESM binding.\n\n### Snapshot parity\n\n- **`remix`: 7 `.snap` files, 50 keys.** They were regenerated from\npristine copies with `vitest run -u`. The value diff after key\nnormalization shows 0 changed, 0 missing and 0 extra.\n`library.impl.spec.ts.snap` kept its obsolete jest-format keys after\n`-u`, because the file contains a skipped test. Those 4 stale keys were\nstripped by hand.\n- **`next`: 2 `.snap` files, 4 keys.** The value diff shows 0 changed, 0\nmissing and 0 extra.\n- **`expo` and `react-native`** have no `.snap` files, and their inline\nsnapshots did not change under `-u`.\n- **Inline snapshot changes** are format-only. Two `remix` inline error\nsnapshots changed from `\"msg\"` to `[Error: msg]`.\n\n### Verification\n\n- `nx run-many -t test,build,lint,oxlint -p remix next expo react-native\ndevkit --skip-nx-cache` passes. These are every runnable target the\nprojects list.\n- `nx sync:check` passes.\n- Two repeat `nx test` runs of all four packages pass with the same\ncounts.\n- The lint warnings in `devkit` and `next` (unused eslint-disable\ndirectives) predate this change.\n\n### Also in this PR: e2e base workspace templates on macOS\n\nThis PR''s macOS e2e run failed in `populate-e2e-base-workspace`: both\npnpm templates broke while being packed (`ENOENT: … lstat\n''…/proj/.git/objects/ea/209a…''`) and removed (`ENOTEMPTY`).\n`create-nx-workspace`''s initial commit can leave enough loose objects\nfor git to start gc/maintenance in the background, which deletes\n`.git/objects` files under the pack. The template builds now run with\n`gc.auto=0` and `maintenance.auto=false` (via `GIT_CONFIG_*`, appended\nto any already set). Locally, a 7,000-file commit had all 7,002 loose\nobjects deleted by background maintenance within seconds by default, and\nnone with these settings. (Previously opened separately as #37197.)\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nN/A\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/Run-rspack-and-rsbuild-unit-tests-with-vitest-4a161f55\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nx-cloud[bot] <71083854+nx-cloud[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqt1ZSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAk68QAIb92z02uh2Z0BpjWytSv/qo\nJH/EiXH0EvpL4Na51zja1uoQrekZMVtN1xdGtkziLYRjyeHiSAIBsr1UY3hese7k\nC8X7vbFuwE/STJ4WydZZ0yws3E8M2l6GmgXXJRYudQfyxpCInfhulVQ8gtv4EpGl\nE86QxwMMmXHH5xQC05XJ1/mk7SITdvkQ9GQvYwsVskFXaNhumhUM/TV1Ql6+3ED+\nf0mBP7RLb/FXegOaGNPN/02IUd5KSd72nTcQWDQ9BsYmns3k4UhCWxwOKKio918Q\n1AsgDUNsIWre4KU/hhTMT4HuCQPd4hNcUkJBmcL/KnxBbEEFF7BFU8gxjg8lKAir\n0gijoMzWRq07G9wISubQbUbZclCV37VHNmfoZzg/pO/6Ix3nt9sSaBtquv5wvTyR\nzN4pcvwEh1toZFxcuBtviO++GXvms19IA+K2DkUiQsZ6N70bPWoBM1AadANmj/FU\n+qu5iY5zgdGog2By/oIFi3dQvqc3wYporAYxcOBA9WS3P/iPi7h2dSwVLzrPYnq8\nCTQ/iyr6iN48QMCjxYfKADtsskV4ARZ1w4fDWKobALnwpu7xZybNDd7qNO/JfJhs\n2z9tcW/KXGPCwU5GZKBeCtfSphl9bi2S/Q1gtfa1JKGpk+JTlj7MmOAHxCFhbZSf\ntyj8tJ8AIu5RFUTkfWD0\n=4F32\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-26T05:21:23Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94","node_id":"C_kwDOBfbSFNoAKGQzZWVlN2QxYjdhN2Y0Y2RmYjBjMzk3ODI1ZDdjMjg1NGUxNDBjOTQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842","sha":"8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842"}],"sha":"d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/d3eee7d1b7a7f4cdfb0c397825d7c2854e140c94"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6933928?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/AgentEnder","id":6933928,"login":"AgentEnder","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjY5MzM5Mjg=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AgentEnder","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-26T03:27:48Z","email":"craigorycoppola@gmail.com","name":"Craigory Coppola"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-26T03:27:48Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(core): opt in to selecting affected tasks instead of projects (#36825)\n\n> Based on `master`. #36841 was rebase-merged into this branch, so it\nnow carries both the locator port and task selection. The 33 commits up\nto `refactor(core): split the affected locators into one file each` are\nthe port; the rest are selection, and the later ones address review —\nsee [Changes from review](#changes-from-review).\n\n\n[NXC-4859](https://linear.app/nxdev/issue/NXC-4859/task-level-nx-affected-backed-by-io-snapshots).\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`nx affected` selects projects, so every target of every dependent runs\nwhether or not the change can reach it. Changing one spec file in\n`packages/nx` selects all 45 `test` tasks here, including projects whose\ntest inputs never see that file.\n\nTwo target configs also create a dependency the project graph never\nrecords:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"inputs\": [{ \"input\": \"production\", \"projects\": [\"shared-config\"] }],\n  \"dependsOn\": [{ \"projects\": [\"api\"], \"target\": \"build\" }],\n}\n```\n\n`gather_project_inputs` inlines the named project''s filesets into the\nconsumer''s plan, and `processTasksForMultipleProjects` builds a task\nedge to it. Neither needs a project-graph edge, so the reverse walk\nmisses both, and a change to a project referenced only this way selects\nnothing.\n\nThe locators themselves run in TypeScript and re-run `minimatch` per\n(pattern, file) pair, recompiling the pattern each call, so their cost\ngrows with changeset size times the number of distinct `{workspaceRoot}`\nfilesets.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nWith `NX_LEGACY_AFFECTED=false`, `nx affected` selects tasks whose\ninputs the change reaches. We build the full task graph for the\nrequested targets, as `run-many` does, resolve each task''s hash plan,\nand match the changed paths against each instruction''s globs. One native\ncall does the matching, resolves which producer''s outputs each task\nreads, and carries the change from producer to consumer by reachability\nover those reads, continuous dependencies included.\n\nThere is no project-grained pass in front of it. That bound rested on\ndeclared ownership, and once I/O snapshots land a task''s observed reads\ncan name a file no project the reverse walk finds would own, so bounding\nby it would miss the task. The two target-config shapes above come along\nfor free, since the referenced project''s filesets are in the consumer''s\nplan.\n\nA lockfile or dependency change reaches a hash as a package name rather\nthan a path, so the JS locators'' diff of it is matched the same way:\neach plan''s `External` instructions against the packages that moved, and\n`AllExternalDependencies` against any.\n`projectsAffectedByDependencyUpdates: \"all\"`, the default, counts every\nnpm package as moved, which selects every task that hashes npm externals\nrather than every task; a Maven or NuGet external cannot have moved in a\nlock file, so a target hashing only those is left alone, as is one with\n`externalDependencies: []`. Only the projects the option names outright\nare still seeded as whole projects.\n\nThis is opt-in. `NX_LEGACY_AFFECTED=false` turns it on; unset, or any\nother value, keeps project selection while the task path settles. The\nvariable is new here, and named for the legacy path so that flipping the\ndefault later leaves `true` as the way to keep project selection. Env\nvar rather than a flag, because a CI script that builds a list with `nx\nshow projects --affected -t build` and then runs `nx affected -t build`\nneeds both commands to agree.\n\n### Selection\n\nChanging one spec file in `packages/nx`:\n\n| command                | legacy | this PR |\n| ---------------------- | ------ | ------- |\n| `-t test`, test tasks  | 45     | 4       |\n| `-t test`, whole graph | 207    | 160     |\n\nThe first row is the win. The rest of each graph is the dependency\nclosure of the selected tasks, which has to stay so the selected ones\ncan run; those restore from cache. `lint` reads nearly every file in a\nproject, so a graph dominated by `lint` narrows very little.\n\nTask selection is not a subset of project selection. A target whose\n`dependsOn` names another project''s target gets an edge the project\ngraph never records; when it also reads that target''s output, task\nselection follows the edge and selects it where the project walk cannot.\nIn this repo `astro-docs:build` does exactly that (`dependsOn: [{\n\"projects\": [\"nx\", \"nuxt\", …], \"target\": \"build\" }]` plus\n`dependentTasksOutputFiles`), so a change under `packages/vue` selects\nit under task selection and not under project selection. That is the\n`dependsOn` shape from Current Behavior coming along for free, not a\ndefect, but it means this setting can run work the default skips.\n\n### `show projects --with-target` follows the granularity\n\nWith a target in hand, `show projects --affected --with-target test`\nanswered \"which affected projects define test\", not \"which projects have\nan affected test task\". The two commands are meant to be chained, so\nthey now answer the same thing:\n\n```\nnx show projects --affected -t test    ->  4\nnx affected -t test                     ->  tasks for those same 4\n```\n\nOnly with a target, and not with `--projects`, which is a name filter\nrather than a target question.\n\n### The locators move to Rust\n\n`getTouchedProjects`, `getImplicitlyTouchedProjects` and\n`getTouchedProjectsFromProjectGlobChanges` are pure path and glob\nmatching and become a native `locateTouchedProjects`, one file each\nunder `native/affected/projects`; task selection lives beside them under\n`native/affected/tasks`. Same results, same locator order, same\nduplicate-emitting contract. The lockfile, npm-package and tsconfig\nlocators stay in TypeScript, since they depend on the lockfile parsers,\nand cross the boundary as `ThreadsafeFunction` callbacks invoked once\nper run. Graph pruning stays in TypeScript too: the native\n`ProjectGraph` has no edge `type` or `source`, so it cannot rebuild what\n`filterAffected` returns.\n\nAlso deletes `workspace-json-changes.ts`, whose `angular.json` locator\nwas already unregistered.\n\nRouting `{workspaceRoot}` filesets through `build_glob_set` instead of\nminimatch changes a few things. The locator now reads an input the way\nthe hasher does, so where the two engines disagree, affected follows the\nhash. These narrow, and in each the file was never part of the task''s\nhash: the first is a shape the hasher rejects, so no task hashing it\never ran, and in the others a selected task only restored from cache:\n\n| fileset | changed file | base | this branch |\n| ---------------------------------------------- |\n------------------------------------ | ------- | -----------------------\n|\n| `{workspaceRoot}/config/*.!(tmp)` | `config/x.json` | matched | no\nmatch |\n| `{workspaceRoot}/apps/web/app/(marketing)/**` |\n`apps/web/app/(marketing)/page.tsx` | matched | no match: `(…)` is a\ngroup |\n| `{workspaceRoot}/v{1..3}/**` | `v2/a.ts` | matched | no match: no\nnumeric ranges |\n\nThe rest are corrections:\n\n- `{workspaceRoot}/!(dist)/**` matched _every_ file under minimatch;\nhere it is a real extglob.\n- `{workspaceRoot}/config/` now matches `config/app.json`, and\n`{workspaceRoot}/some-dir/` becomes `some-dir/**`.\n- A leading `#` is a literal character rather than a minimatch comment.\n- The \"all projects\" answer and the implicit-fileset hits come back\nsorted, so `nx show projects --affected` stops reordering between runs.\n- A fileset named `{workspaceRoot}/__proto__` or `constructor` is no\nlonger dropped by the old `implicits[input] ??= []`.\n- A self-referencing named input terminates instead of overflowing the\nstack.\n- One plugin''s `createNodes` glob failing to parse no longer switches\ndeletion detection off for every plugin.\n- An implicit fileset is read as the hasher reads it: repeated slashes\ncollapse, a literal path covers everything under it\n(`{workspaceRoot}/generated` matches `generated/api.ts`), and a glob the\nparser rejects is matched literally, as minimatch did, rather than\ndropped.\n\n### Cost\n\nPlanning every task of the targets is the price of dropping the bound.\nIt runs once per invocation, about 3ms per task here, and the run does\nnot plan again: the planner remembers whole task plans and reuses one\nwhile that task''s target, outputs and edges, and those of everything it\ndepends on, are unchanged, plus the I/O snapshot set. Selection and\nhashing share one planner, in the client with the daemon off and in the\ndaemon, one per project graph, with it on, so the run''s hashing is\nanswered from what selection planned. Matching is bounded by the change\nrather than the workspace: the changed files are indexed by owning\nproject once, so an instruction scoped to a project the change never\ntouched is answered without compiling its globs. On a synthetic pool of\n10k projects with three filesets each, a one-file change matches in 4ms.\nA file instruction is ruled out by its globs'' literal leading folders\nbefore any glob is compiled, which takes a 2,000-project workspace with\na per-project `includeIgnored` set from 35ms to 2–3ms on a cold run.\nTask hashes are unchanged except under `selectivelyHashTsConfig` for a\nproject whose root is empty or ends in `/`: the native root mapping\nnever found such a project, so its own `paths` entries were missing from\nits hash; they are now included, which changes those tasks'' hashes once.\n\nThe locators on a synthetic 3000-project graph with many distinct\nfilesets, min of 11:\n\n| changed files | before   | after       |\n| ------------- | -------- | ----------- |\n| 10            | 373 ms   | **65 ms**   |\n| 1000          | 12824 ms | **1239 ms** |\n\nWith few, shared filesets a one-file change goes from 9 ms to 40 ms, the\nfixed cost of marshalling the graph natively. The locators get a slim\ncopy holding only what they read: project roots, named inputs and target\ninputs. On a synthetic 5,000-project workspace that is about half the\ncost of a full copy. Selection, the planner and the hasher share one\nfull copy, since they read external nodes. Each copy is cached per graph\nidentity, so a run pays for it once.\n\n### Changes from review\n\n- **Propagation is reachability, not an ordered sweep.** The sweep\nvisited tasks in an order built from regular dependencies only, so a\nconsumer reached only through a continuous dependency (an e2e suite on a\nserved app) was visited before its producer and never selected, and\nunder a cycle the order fell back to task names. Both are gone: the walk\nfloods outward from the touched tasks and needs no order.\n- **A `TaskOutput` is resolved across continuous dependencies too.** The\nplanner splices a served task''s reads into its consumer''s plan, so an\ne2e suite can read a library''s build through the serve task. Edge\nresolution now follows both edge kinds for every read.\n- **An unpinned dependency change stays in its own ecosystem.**\n`allExternals` became `changedExternalTypes`, and the matcher checks\neach node''s type, which the native graph now carries\n(`ExternalNode.type`). No task hash changes: `hash_external` reads only\nversion and hash. A node with no type matches no ecosystem.\n- **`--graph` draws what the run executes.** The run''s prune is a shared\nhelper, used by the live graph, HTML, JSON and `stdout` output alike.\nThe `-c` configuration reaches every one of them too: a task id carries\nit, so a graph built without it would prune to nothing.\n- **Errors under the opt-in** are reported by the CLI''s top-level\nhandler, like the default path''s: selection, including planning, which\nthrows on an `externalDependencies` entry naming no external node, runs\nbefore `nx affected`''s own error handler, with the error''s message as\nthe title.\n- **Docs**: the affected page describes the opt-in, that a plain\n`dependsOn` does not carry affectedness, the `cache: false` caveat, and\nthat task selection can select what project selection misses.\n- **Plans are reused with the daemon on.** Selection runs in the daemon\n(`SELECT_AFFECTED_TASKS`) with the same planner the daemon hashes with,\nso the run''s hashing reuses its plans. `show projects --affected` goes\nthrough the same path, so chaining it into `affected` plans once. The\ndaemon returns the project graph it selected against, and the command\nruns with it, so the two cannot disagree.\n- **Tests**: `e2e/nx/src/affected-tasks.test.ts` covers the opt-in end\nto end with the daemon on, alongside the existing default-path e2e. The\nopt-in default and the untyped-node rule are pinned.\n- **Only the requested targets are reported.** Selection still plans\nwhat those targets depend on so a change can travel through it, but a\ndependency-only task, like a `prebuild` under `-t build`, runs only when\nan affected task needs it, and `show projects` no longer lists its\nproject.\n- **Selection returns what the run keeps.** The affected tasks and their\ndependency closure come back from the native call, over both edge kinds.\n`--exclude` is applied there, after propagation, so an excluded task\nstill carries a change and still runs when a kept task needs it.\n- **The run reuses selection''s task graph.** Selection records the\noverrides each `dependsOn` edge would give a task and filters its graph\nto what the run keeps (`filterTaskGraphToSelection`), giving each\ndependency-only task the overrides the run would; the runner and every\n`--graph` output use it instead of building again. When the edges into a\ntask disagree, the run builds as before. The runner now takes a\n`TaskSelection` (the graph, the initiating task ids, and what a graph\nrebuilt after sync is pruned to) rather than projects, so `run`,\n`run-many` and `release` build theirs the same way they always have and\nhand it over. `--graph` builds the selection lazily, so a selection\nerror shows in the graph rather than stopping it.\n- **Plans are remembered by the planner, per task.** A task whose\nclosure changed between selection and the run, such as a dependency\nwhose overrides moved its outputs, is planned again; the rest are\nreused. This replaces a whole-set check that replanned everything on any\ndifference.\n- **Selection plans with the run''s I/O snapshot set.** The command loads\nthe set once, selection plans with it (in the daemon by version) and\napplies the observed outputs, and the run hashes with the same set, so a\nchange to a file a task read but never declared selects it (#37036).\n- **A task whose executor has a custom hasher is always selected**,\nsince it hashes outside its plan.\n- **An `includeIgnored` read may read any output it could overlap.**\nReads and outputs are compared by their literal leading folders, taken\nfrom the glob parser (`partition_glob`, so an escaped bracket stays in\nthe folder name), and by a literal extension after a wildcard. Repeated\nslashes collapse as disk expansion collapses them, in the matcher and in\nthis check.\n- **`--exclude-task-dependencies` no longer hides a change.** Selection\nruns over the full task graph and the flag trims only what runs, so a\nconsumer still reaches its producer''s change through a dependency it\ndoes not run.\n- **Field-filtered JSON inputs and the root tsconfig select by what the\nhasher reads.** A JSON input with `fields`/`excludeFields` is touched\nonly by a change inside a kept field; native reads such a file at both\nrevisions on first use and parses it with the hasher''s own JSON/JSONC\nparser. For the root tsconfig, a change outside `compilerOptions.paths`\n(key order included, as `JSON.stringify` sees it) touches every\n`TsConfiguration`; a `paths`-only change touches only the projects whose\nown entries changed under `selectivelyHashTsConfig`, and none without\nit. Moving the `typescript` package touches every tsconfig input, as the\nhasher reads it.\n- **One JSON diff, in Rust.** Project mode''s `calculateFileChanges`\ndiffs a `.json` file with native `diffJson`, the same core task\nselection reads field-filtered inputs and the root tsconfig with, and\n`jsonDiff` (YAML and the locator specs) wraps it; the TS walk is gone.\nIt keeps `jsonDiff`''s changes and their order, and is strict like\n`JSON.parse`. Over 384 consecutive revision pairs of this repo''s\n`package.json`, `tsconfig.base.json` and `nx.json` files the two diffs\nare identical. As in JSON, an `undefined` value now counts as no key.\n- **A changed file''s working-tree version is read from the workspace\nroot**, not the current directory, so `nx affected` from a subfolder no\nlonger treats lock files and JSON as changed whole.\n- **Release''s version-plan check uses the native direct-owner lookup**\n(`directlyTouchedProjects`) rather than a TypeScript copy of it.\n\nFollow-up, not in this PR:\n[NXC-5036](https://linear.app/nxdev/issue/NXC-5036) (a hash plan cannot\nexpress that its externals are unknown),\n[NXC-5048](https://linear.app/nxdev/issue/NXC-5048) (selection isn''t\nredone after a sync generator applies changes), and the tsconfig hasher\nlooking up an external node named `typescript` where npm nodes are\n`npm:typescript`.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nNXC-4859\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/task-based-affected-graph-f381a984\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nx-cloud[bot] <71083854+nx-cloud[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: FrozenPandaz <FrozenPandaz@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com>","tree":{"sha":"13f64067877b0cd8b8f0f94552340ec7d2eb652b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/trees/13f64067877b0cd8b8f0f94552340ec7d2eb652b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/commits/8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842","verification":{"payload":"tree 13f64067877b0cd8b8f0f94552340ec7d2eb652b\nparent 0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1\nauthor Craigory Coppola <craigorycoppola@gmail.com> 1790393268 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790393268 -0400\n\nfeat(core): opt in to selecting affected tasks instead of projects (#36825)\n\n> Based on `master`. #36841 was rebase-merged into this branch, so it\nnow carries both the locator port and task selection. The 33 commits up\nto `refactor(core): split the affected locators into one file each` are\nthe port; the rest are selection, and the later ones address review —\nsee [Changes from review](#changes-from-review).\n\n\n[NXC-4859](https://linear.app/nxdev/issue/NXC-4859/task-level-nx-affected-backed-by-io-snapshots).\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`nx affected` selects projects, so every target of every dependent runs\nwhether or not the change can reach it. Changing one spec file in\n`packages/nx` selects all 45 `test` tasks here, including projects whose\ntest inputs never see that file.\n\nTwo target configs also create a dependency the project graph never\nrecords:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"inputs\": [{ \"input\": \"production\", \"projects\": [\"shared-config\"] }],\n  \"dependsOn\": [{ \"projects\": [\"api\"], \"target\": \"build\" }],\n}\n```\n\n`gather_project_inputs` inlines the named project''s filesets into the\nconsumer''s plan, and `processTasksForMultipleProjects` builds a task\nedge to it. Neither needs a project-graph edge, so the reverse walk\nmisses both, and a change to a project referenced only this way selects\nnothing.\n\nThe locators themselves run in TypeScript and re-run `minimatch` per\n(pattern, file) pair, recompiling the pattern each call, so their cost\ngrows with changeset size times the number of distinct `{workspaceRoot}`\nfilesets.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nWith `NX_LEGACY_AFFECTED=false`, `nx affected` selects tasks whose\ninputs the change reaches. We build the full task graph for the\nrequested targets, as `run-many` does, resolve each task''s hash plan,\nand match the changed paths against each instruction''s globs. One native\ncall does the matching, resolves which producer''s outputs each task\nreads, and carries the change from producer to consumer by reachability\nover those reads, continuous dependencies included.\n\nThere is no project-grained pass in front of it. That bound rested on\ndeclared ownership, and once I/O snapshots land a task''s observed reads\ncan name a file no project the reverse walk finds would own, so bounding\nby it would miss the task. The two target-config shapes above come along\nfor free, since the referenced project''s filesets are in the consumer''s\nplan.\n\nA lockfile or dependency change reaches a hash as a package name rather\nthan a path, so the JS locators'' diff of it is matched the same way:\neach plan''s `External` instructions against the packages that moved, and\n`AllExternalDependencies` against any.\n`projectsAffectedByDependencyUpdates: \"all\"`, the default, counts every\nnpm package as moved, which selects every task that hashes npm externals\nrather than every task; a Maven or NuGet external cannot have moved in a\nlock file, so a target hashing only those is left alone, as is one with\n`externalDependencies: []`. Only the projects the option names outright\nare still seeded as whole projects.\n\nThis is opt-in. `NX_LEGACY_AFFECTED=false` turns it on; unset, or any\nother value, keeps project selection while the task path settles. The\nvariable is new here, and named for the legacy path so that flipping the\ndefault later leaves `true` as the way to keep project selection. Env\nvar rather than a flag, because a CI script that builds a list with `nx\nshow projects --affected -t build` and then runs `nx affected -t build`\nneeds both commands to agree.\n\n### Selection\n\nChanging one spec file in `packages/nx`:\n\n| command                | legacy | this PR |\n| ---------------------- | ------ | ------- |\n| `-t test`, test tasks  | 45     | 4       |\n| `-t test`, whole graph | 207    | 160     |\n\nThe first row is the win. The rest of each graph is the dependency\nclosure of the selected tasks, which has to stay so the selected ones\ncan run; those restore from cache. `lint` reads nearly every file in a\nproject, so a graph dominated by `lint` narrows very little.\n\nTask selection is not a subset of project selection. A target whose\n`dependsOn` names another project''s target gets an edge the project\ngraph never records; when it also reads that target''s output, task\nselection follows the edge and selects it where the project walk cannot.\nIn this repo `astro-docs:build` does exactly that (`dependsOn: [{\n\"projects\": [\"nx\", \"nuxt\", …], \"target\": \"build\" }]` plus\n`dependentTasksOutputFiles`), so a change under `packages/vue` selects\nit under task selection and not under project selection. That is the\n`dependsOn` shape from Current Behavior coming along for free, not a\ndefect, but it means this setting can run work the default skips.\n\n### `show projects --with-target` follows the granularity\n\nWith a target in hand, `show projects --affected --with-target test`\nanswered \"which affected projects define test\", not \"which projects have\nan affected test task\". The two commands are meant to be chained, so\nthey now answer the same thing:\n\n```\nnx show projects --affected -t test    ->  4\nnx affected -t test                     ->  tasks for those same 4\n```\n\nOnly with a target, and not with `--projects`, which is a name filter\nrather than a target question.\n\n### The locators move to Rust\n\n`getTouchedProjects`, `getImplicitlyTouchedProjects` and\n`getTouchedProjectsFromProjectGlobChanges` are pure path and glob\nmatching and become a native `locateTouchedProjects`, one file each\nunder `native/affected/projects`; task selection lives beside them under\n`native/affected/tasks`. Same results, same locator order, same\nduplicate-emitting contract. The lockfile, npm-package and tsconfig\nlocators stay in TypeScript, since they depend on the lockfile parsers,\nand cross the boundary as `ThreadsafeFunction` callbacks invoked once\nper run. Graph pruning stays in TypeScript too: the native\n`ProjectGraph` has no edge `type` or `source`, so it cannot rebuild what\n`filterAffected` returns.\n\nAlso deletes `workspace-json-changes.ts`, whose `angular.json` locator\nwas already unregistered.\n\nRouting `{workspaceRoot}` filesets through `build_glob_set` instead of\nminimatch changes a few things. The locator now reads an input the way\nthe hasher does, so where the two engines disagree, affected follows the\nhash. These narrow, and in each the file was never part of the task''s\nhash: the first is a shape the hasher rejects, so no task hashing it\never ran, and in the others a selected task only restored from cache:\n\n| fileset | changed file | base | this branch |\n| ---------------------------------------------- |\n------------------------------------ | ------- | -----------------------\n|\n| `{workspaceRoot}/config/*.!(tmp)` | `config/x.json` | matched | no\nmatch |\n| `{workspaceRoot}/apps/web/app/(marketing)/**` |\n`apps/web/app/(marketing)/page.tsx` | matched | no match: `(…)` is a\ngroup |\n| `{workspaceRoot}/v{1..3}/**` | `v2/a.ts` | matched | no match: no\nnumeric ranges |\n\nThe rest are corrections:\n\n- `{workspaceRoot}/!(dist)/**` matched _every_ file under minimatch;\nhere it is a real extglob.\n- `{workspaceRoot}/config/` now matches `config/app.json`, and\n`{workspaceRoot}/some-dir/` becomes `some-dir/**`.\n- A leading `#` is a literal character rather than a minimatch comment.\n- The \"all projects\" answer and the implicit-fileset hits come back\nsorted, so `nx show projects --affected` stops reordering between runs.\n- A fileset named `{workspaceRoot}/__proto__` or `constructor` is no\nlonger dropped by the old `implicits[input] ??= []`.\n- A self-referencing named input terminates instead of overflowing the\nstack.\n- One plugin''s `createNodes` glob failing to parse no longer switches\ndeletion detection off for every plugin.\n- An implicit fileset is read as the hasher reads it: repeated slashes\ncollapse, a literal path covers everything under it\n(`{workspaceRoot}/generated` matches `generated/api.ts`), and a glob the\nparser rejects is matched literally, as minimatch did, rather than\ndropped.\n\n### Cost\n\nPlanning every task of the targets is the price of dropping the bound.\nIt runs once per invocation, about 3ms per task here, and the run does\nnot plan again: the planner remembers whole task plans and reuses one\nwhile that task''s target, outputs and edges, and those of everything it\ndepends on, are unchanged, plus the I/O snapshot set. Selection and\nhashing share one planner, in the client with the daemon off and in the\ndaemon, one per project graph, with it on, so the run''s hashing is\nanswered from what selection planned. Matching is bounded by the change\nrather than the workspace: the changed files are indexed by owning\nproject once, so an instruction scoped to a project the change never\ntouched is answered without compiling its globs. On a synthetic pool of\n10k projects with three filesets each, a one-file change matches in 4ms.\nA file instruction is ruled out by its globs'' literal leading folders\nbefore any glob is compiled, which takes a 2,000-project workspace with\na per-project `includeIgnored` set from 35ms to 2–3ms on a cold run.\nTask hashes are unchanged except under `selectivelyHashTsConfig` for a\nproject whose root is empty or ends in `/`: the native root mapping\nnever found such a project, so its own `paths` entries were missing from\nits hash; they are now included, which changes those tasks'' hashes once.\n\nThe locators on a synthetic 3000-project graph with many distinct\nfilesets, min of 11:\n\n| changed files | before   | after       |\n| ------------- | -------- | ----------- |\n| 10            | 373 ms   | **65 ms**   |\n| 1000          | 12824 ms | **1239 ms** |\n\nWith few, shared filesets a one-file change goes from 9 ms to 40 ms, the\nfixed cost of marshalling the graph natively. The locators get a slim\ncopy holding only what they read: project roots, named inputs and target\ninputs. On a synthetic 5,000-project workspace that is about half the\ncost of a full copy. Selection, the planner and the hasher share one\nfull copy, since they read external nodes. Each copy is cached per graph\nidentity, so a run pays for it once.\n\n### Changes from review\n\n- **Propagation is reachability, not an ordered sweep.** The sweep\nvisited tasks in an order built from regular dependencies only, so a\nconsumer reached only through a continuous dependency (an e2e suite on a\nserved app) was visited before its producer and never selected, and\nunder a cycle the order fell back to task names. Both are gone: the walk\nfloods outward from the touched tasks and needs no order.\n- **A `TaskOutput` is resolved across continuous dependencies too.** The\nplanner splices a served task''s reads into its consumer''s plan, so an\ne2e suite can read a library''s build through the serve task. Edge\nresolution now follows both edge kinds for every read.\n- **An unpinned dependency change stays in its own ecosystem.**\n`allExternals` became `changedExternalTypes`, and the matcher checks\neach node''s type, which the native graph now carries\n(`ExternalNode.type`). No task hash changes: `hash_external` reads only\nversion and hash. A node with no type matches no ecosystem.\n- **`--graph` draws what the run executes.** The run''s prune is a shared\nhelper, used by the live graph, HTML, JSON and `stdout` output alike.\nThe `-c` configuration reaches every one of them too: a task id carries\nit, so a graph built without it would prune to nothing.\n- **Errors under the opt-in** are reported by the CLI''s top-level\nhandler, like the default path''s: selection, including planning, which\nthrows on an `externalDependencies` entry naming no external node, runs\nbefore `nx affected`''s own error handler, with the error''s message as\nthe title.\n- **Docs**: the affected page describes the opt-in, that a plain\n`dependsOn` does not carry affectedness, the `cache: false` caveat, and\nthat task selection can select what project selection misses.\n- **Plans are reused with the daemon on.** Selection runs in the daemon\n(`SELECT_AFFECTED_TASKS`) with the same planner the daemon hashes with,\nso the run''s hashing reuses its plans. `show projects --affected` goes\nthrough the same path, so chaining it into `affected` plans once. The\ndaemon returns the project graph it selected against, and the command\nruns with it, so the two cannot disagree.\n- **Tests**: `e2e/nx/src/affected-tasks.test.ts` covers the opt-in end\nto end with the daemon on, alongside the existing default-path e2e. The\nopt-in default and the untyped-node rule are pinned.\n- **Only the requested targets are reported.** Selection still plans\nwhat those targets depend on so a change can travel through it, but a\ndependency-only task, like a `prebuild` under `-t build`, runs only when\nan affected task needs it, and `show projects` no longer lists its\nproject.\n- **Selection returns what the run keeps.** The affected tasks and their\ndependency closure come back from the native call, over both edge kinds.\n`--exclude` is applied there, after propagation, so an excluded task\nstill carries a change and still runs when a kept task needs it.\n- **The run reuses selection''s task graph.** Selection records the\noverrides each `dependsOn` edge would give a task and filters its graph\nto what the run keeps (`filterTaskGraphToSelection`), giving each\ndependency-only task the overrides the run would; the runner and every\n`--graph` output use it instead of building again. When the edges into a\ntask disagree, the run builds as before. The runner now takes a\n`TaskSelection` (the graph, the initiating task ids, and what a graph\nrebuilt after sync is pruned to) rather than projects, so `run`,\n`run-many` and `release` build theirs the same way they always have and\nhand it over. `--graph` builds the selection lazily, so a selection\nerror shows in the graph rather than stopping it.\n- **Plans are remembered by the planner, per task.** A task whose\nclosure changed between selection and the run, such as a dependency\nwhose overrides moved its outputs, is planned again; the rest are\nreused. This replaces a whole-set check that replanned everything on any\ndifference.\n- **Selection plans with the run''s I/O snapshot set.** The command loads\nthe set once, selection plans with it (in the daemon by version) and\napplies the observed outputs, and the run hashes with the same set, so a\nchange to a file a task read but never declared selects it (#37036).\n- **A task whose executor has a custom hasher is always selected**,\nsince it hashes outside its plan.\n- **An `includeIgnored` read may read any output it could overlap.**\nReads and outputs are compared by their literal leading folders, taken\nfrom the glob parser (`partition_glob`, so an escaped bracket stays in\nthe folder name), and by a literal extension after a wildcard. Repeated\nslashes collapse as disk expansion collapses them, in the matcher and in\nthis check.\n- **`--exclude-task-dependencies` no longer hides a change.** Selection\nruns over the full task graph and the flag trims only what runs, so a\nconsumer still reaches its producer''s change through a dependency it\ndoes not run.\n- **Field-filtered JSON inputs and the root tsconfig select by what the\nhasher reads.** A JSON input with `fields`/`excludeFields` is touched\nonly by a change inside a kept field; native reads such a file at both\nrevisions on first use and parses it with the hasher''s own JSON/JSONC\nparser. For the root tsconfig, a change outside `compilerOptions.paths`\n(key order included, as `JSON.stringify` sees it) touches every\n`TsConfiguration`; a `paths`-only change touches only the projects whose\nown entries changed under `selectivelyHashTsConfig`, and none without\nit. Moving the `typescript` package touches every tsconfig input, as the\nhasher reads it.\n- **One JSON diff, in Rust.** Project mode''s `calculateFileChanges`\ndiffs a `.json` file with native `diffJson`, the same core task\nselection reads field-filtered inputs and the root tsconfig with, and\n`jsonDiff` (YAML and the locator specs) wraps it; the TS walk is gone.\nIt keeps `jsonDiff`''s changes and their order, and is strict like\n`JSON.parse`. Over 384 consecutive revision pairs of this repo''s\n`package.json`, `tsconfig.base.json` and `nx.json` files the two diffs\nare identical. As in JSON, an `undefined` value now counts as no key.\n- **A changed file''s working-tree version is read from the workspace\nroot**, not the current directory, so `nx affected` from a subfolder no\nlonger treats lock files and JSON as changed whole.\n- **Release''s version-plan check uses the native direct-owner lookup**\n(`directlyTouchedProjects`) rather than a TypeScript copy of it.\n\nFollow-up, not in this PR:\n[NXC-5036](https://linear.app/nxdev/issue/NXC-5036) (a hash plan cannot\nexpress that its externals are unknown),\n[NXC-5048](https://linear.app/nxdev/issue/NXC-5048) (selection isn''t\nredone after a sync generator applies changes), and the tsconfig hasher\nlooking up an external node named `typescript` where npm nodes are\n`npm:typescript`.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nNXC-4859\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/task-based-affected-graph-f381a984\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nx-cloud[bot] <71083854+nx-cloud[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: FrozenPandaz <FrozenPandaz@users.noreply.github.com>\nCo-authored-by: Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtzu0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA+K8QABab6CVLyifHuXXaAUqxYPpF\nxmhk6nyMLnD778SC/4TYGMQx3ae0R178LZNkFRCyVbgeZY+ZHSRzRsNG47F7lyl0\na/x9jIY3CL74xihRvecgiBxv45K8YmahBUY4CmV3rOpmHd0DZLHle9AwUUYRb+Qg\nuCn5962tD0Fqw7tNNCp7gHFbE7gwyoiGaSXfweFiDkmaIuSZS49NKkQhuJr1FuLs\nqNptM7BzeipCGKU5+/KeQAk8mVbfqjltZTZSGWJxTDv9SoL1ozmIYYs4f8mBL9Tv\nqJoY3NYtsLUJx/c535f69UKbTjIVrdAmL8+xOcm4Idl/b3JN70z94efL+OpXWrkE\nLXIrY48di2EvcLmC35RuL3p+/VXrOas3HLOIJFrWHLe8rIlessdZWuEy/jc6O/52\npwHKZVb1A4JnEdsGWbt5XxdFDx9YCUnNpC6PYzDtyPCfS1WQMoXZUFg3eAn/RDZJ\nihWjHYAtiSrKT4VLg4dwPgh4/s5hynz5fGnOPb41k4kP0+zazNp45iLxLsJ3kqpj\nKpsDkkNUsgd/Mp2L/AGB2y1boRsUwd/mhtOcno7crLxrVoeZv3Es/KCLAyKBjCzb\nirlxbCA62vEQhc3r3ri411/iAfhGjZ+zPqagwbHrkWdBgteWZXI5TWr2WSo6X/K+\nT/3EwxpQVhID5H9x7a+o\n=wpeY\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-26T03:27:49Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842","node_id":"C_kwDOBfbSFNoAKDhjOTkzYWFlMzViMzUyY2EwZDA3NzVlMThkOGQ1YzQyY2NmYjE4NDI","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1","sha":"0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1"}],"sha":"8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/8c993aae35b352ca0d0775e18d8d5c42ccfb1842"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8104246?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/FrozenPandaz","id":8104246,"login":"FrozenPandaz","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMDQyNDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T18:57:09Z","email":"jasonjean1993@gmail.com","name":"Jason Jean"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T18:57:09Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(core): read glob syntax from the glob parser in one place (#37169)\n\n## Current Behavior\n\nSeveral parts of the native glob code each keep their own list of\n\"special\" characters to decide where the literal part of a glob ends.\nThe lists disagree with each other and with the glob parser:\n\n- `contains_glob_pattern` flags ordinary directory names as globs:\n`@scope`, `a+b`, `co,ma`, `pi|pe`, `x)`, `y]`. Output handling only gets\naway with this because it checks the disk first.\n- `partition_glob`, `common_glob_prefix` and `glob_hashing::classify`\neach use a different character list and different cleanup rules.\n- `parse_glob` silently truncates a glob when a segment ends with a lone\n`?`, `+`, `@` or `!`, or has an unclosed group. `convert_glob(\"a/?/b\")`\nreturns `[\"a\"]`, and `truncated/foo+/**/!(ignored).ts` is matched as the\nsingle file `truncated`, with no error.\n- `libs/a\\ b/x` is split with the escape left in the directory name, so\nthe walk looks for a folder literally named `a\\ b`.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe glob parser is the single place that knows glob syntax:\n\n- `parse_glob` now also returns globset''s own syntax as separate parts,\nnamed after globset''s `Token`s: `Literal`, `Recursive`, `Wildcard`,\n`Any`, `Class`, `Alternates` and `Escaped`. Each part prints back to its\nsource text, so `convert_glob` output is unchanged. A round-trip test\nover the corpus proves this.\n- `literal_segment` gives a segment''s real name (escapes resolved), or\nnothing when it is a pattern. `partition_glob`, `common_glob_prefix` and\n`classify` all use it, so `@scope`, `a+b` and `co,ma` are plain names,\nand `libs/\\*/x` has the root `libs/*/x`. A stray `}` counts as syntax,\nbecause globset rejects it.\n- `contains_glob_pattern` is removed. `expand_outputs` and\n`validate_outputs` read entries with `partition_glob`. A literal output\nentry is read as written first, then by its escape-resolved name, so a\nreal `dist\\app` directory still works off Windows.\n- `partition_glob` is a pure split and no longer trims a trailing `/`.\nCleanup is left to `normalize_glob`, which `common_glob_prefix` now\ncalls, so workspace glob narrowing also covers `libs//a/**` and\n`libs/?(a)/x`.\n- A glob is either read whole or refused: a trailing `?`, `+`, `@` or\n`!` is read as itself, and an unclosed group is an error rather than a\nshorter glob. With that, `build_glob_set` converts every glob, and the\n\"only convert when it contains `!|({,`\" guard is gone.\n- Project matching does not error on a pattern that is not a valid glob:\n`tag:wip(` still matches a project tagged `wip(` exactly, as it\neffectively did before.\n- `test-native` inputs include `src/native/**/fixtures/**/*`, since the\ncorpus is embedded with `include_str!`.\n\nA corpus test (about 100 hand-picked globs covering each kind of syntax,\nplus real plugin globs) pins the answers of `convert_glob`,\n`partition_glob` and the narrowing prefix. Each commit''s behavior change\nis visible as a diff of that snapshot:\n\n- The only changed answers are names that are no longer flagged as\nglobs, backslash escapes, and previously truncated globs.\n- Match results do not change. The narrowing prefix changes only where\nthe root is now more precise, and every file the glob matches is still\nunder it.\n- Removing the convert guard changes nothing in the snapshot.\n\nThe corpus snapshot is recorded off Windows, where `\\` is an escape, so\nit does not run on Windows.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nFixes NXC-5007\nFixes NXC-5001\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/One-glob-syntax-reader-parser-backed-literal-checks-NXC-5007-NXC-5001-fb2c38c1\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nx-cloud[bot] <71083854+nx-cloud[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"31e2f787f40897a4991fc1f9a2be56944e9349a7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/trees/31e2f787f40897a4991fc1f9a2be56944e9349a7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/commits/0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1","verification":{"payload":"tree 31e2f787f40897a4991fc1f9a2be56944e9349a7\nparent 30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689\nauthor Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com> 1790362629 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790362629 +0000\n\nfix(core): read glob syntax from the glob parser in one place (#37169)\n\n## Current Behavior\n\nSeveral parts of the native glob code each keep their own list of\n\"special\" characters to decide where the literal part of a glob ends.\nThe lists disagree with each other and with the glob parser:\n\n- `contains_glob_pattern` flags ordinary directory names as globs:\n`@scope`, `a+b`, `co,ma`, `pi|pe`, `x)`, `y]`. Output handling only gets\naway with this because it checks the disk first.\n- `partition_glob`, `common_glob_prefix` and `glob_hashing::classify`\neach use a different character list and different cleanup rules.\n- `parse_glob` silently truncates a glob when a segment ends with a lone\n`?`, `+`, `@` or `!`, or has an unclosed group. `convert_glob(\"a/?/b\")`\nreturns `[\"a\"]`, and `truncated/foo+/**/!(ignored).ts` is matched as the\nsingle file `truncated`, with no error.\n- `libs/a\\ b/x` is split with the escape left in the directory name, so\nthe walk looks for a folder literally named `a\\ b`.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe glob parser is the single place that knows glob syntax:\n\n- `parse_glob` now also returns globset''s own syntax as separate parts,\nnamed after globset''s `Token`s: `Literal`, `Recursive`, `Wildcard`,\n`Any`, `Class`, `Alternates` and `Escaped`. Each part prints back to its\nsource text, so `convert_glob` output is unchanged. A round-trip test\nover the corpus proves this.\n- `literal_segment` gives a segment''s real name (escapes resolved), or\nnothing when it is a pattern. `partition_glob`, `common_glob_prefix` and\n`classify` all use it, so `@scope`, `a+b` and `co,ma` are plain names,\nand `libs/\\*/x` has the root `libs/*/x`. A stray `}` counts as syntax,\nbecause globset rejects it.\n- `contains_glob_pattern` is removed. `expand_outputs` and\n`validate_outputs` read entries with `partition_glob`. A literal output\nentry is read as written first, then by its escape-resolved name, so a\nreal `dist\\app` directory still works off Windows.\n- `partition_glob` is a pure split and no longer trims a trailing `/`.\nCleanup is left to `normalize_glob`, which `common_glob_prefix` now\ncalls, so workspace glob narrowing also covers `libs//a/**` and\n`libs/?(a)/x`.\n- A glob is either read whole or refused: a trailing `?`, `+`, `@` or\n`!` is read as itself, and an unclosed group is an error rather than a\nshorter glob. With that, `build_glob_set` converts every glob, and the\n\"only convert when it contains `!|({,`\" guard is gone.\n- Project matching does not error on a pattern that is not a valid glob:\n`tag:wip(` still matches a project tagged `wip(` exactly, as it\neffectively did before.\n- `test-native` inputs include `src/native/**/fixtures/**/*`, since the\ncorpus is embedded with `include_str!`.\n\nA corpus test (about 100 hand-picked globs covering each kind of syntax,\nplus real plugin globs) pins the answers of `convert_glob`,\n`partition_glob` and the narrowing prefix. Each commit''s behavior change\nis visible as a diff of that snapshot:\n\n- The only changed answers are names that are no longer flagged as\nglobs, backslash escapes, and previously truncated globs.\n- Match results do not change. The narrowing prefix changes only where\nthe root is now more precise, and every file the glob matches is still\nunder it.\n- Removing the convert guard changes nothing in the snapshot.\n\nThe corpus snapshot is recorded off Windows, where `\\` is an escape, so\nit does not run on Windows.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nFixes NXC-5007\nFixes NXC-5001\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/One-glob-syntax-reader-parser-backed-literal-checks-NXC-5007-NXC-5001-fb2c38c1\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: nx-cloud[bot] <71083854+nx-cloud[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtsQFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAl/MQAABLp6h2WzZBYcq7CC56YhEo\ndFnOmV80g0N+eDW4RSYtFn5v2la04ECMpsPuOWLTU13tx+S6Zcqcsj1cbxaYTdxR\nQc2m76NNGXKdVmzleZXOXgWz6F8R6PkMcgaTVCthccY8xnO6zJpssDlMqj5JNoL0\nGKeI+hA9b+B8OSsDTHQM8hWFcUv/QuXbbZ4Rcd2YI0KF5F1ZKyy7otLRNAYXpdG+\nl0oDJdOxombu8avCl8s9O6Pa62r1Y6xsVyLmK6d4drji1QBk5DNA1RZNObRLDn58\n0leV9ycFqHvwDeytCbFzbfqI4Pp/RS1AKpUwR2PH3sgTCGVUFVwQvnBLmZ9MZl9U\nbAuQ3CuIu2yqrEAAh7i/XbkfJ2ohNlamIemO3WAlkyGZwr6J14pjwYs2ic2EQQt8\ncRTar1rivKx9WIvdZi2UjC+8GAp1qYiLABRSnyLcQcZmGMIxX/XHD8IrW6rSWxqG\n+fCez/J37RzGYr+cZ3lMnyezrXds52WKo7WyDD6jEsB3Wi3u2v0f2kWpdERNGNCs\nhvvbVULHEE/Th71GsDAIIMjzNJ4Ky0oI+KkjHYJSutZOJheH83VpLtT4QyfvUGyV\ngrwoFo0Kn+bn/YvSyFKlUdpxhzq/0TZejVz4bBuFJnTCP4r3pn2z7DuVhUcJWqWj\ncrzhJ3PirogGnnPsu6kk\n=aGE6\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T18:57:10Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1","node_id":"C_kwDOBfbSFNoAKDBkOGZjYTFiMjQ1Y2UyNzAwYjI0MDAwODE0MGQzZWFlZjQ5ZWViYjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689","sha":"30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689"}],"sha":"0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/0d8fca1b245ce2700b240008140d3eaef49eebb1"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8104246?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/FrozenPandaz","id":8104246,"login":"FrozenPandaz","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMDQyNDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T17:35:31Z","email":"jasonjean1993@gmail.com","name":"Jason Jean"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T17:35:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(repo): make e2e base workspace template failures clean and uncached (#37194)\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`populate-e2e-base-workspace`\n(`scripts/local-registry/build-e2e-base-workspace.mjs`) can crash\noutright, as it just did on `main-macos` for #37173:\n\n```\nWrote base workspace template: .../proj-backup/bun-apps.tar\nWrote base workspace template: .../proj-backup/bun-ts.tar\nnode:internal/process/promises:324\n    triggerUncaughtException(err, true /* fromPromise */);\nError [ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE]: Premature close\n    at Pack.destroy (tar-stream/pack.js:203:8)\n    at Sink.<anonymous> (tar-stream/pack.js:174:12)\n```\n\n`pack.js:174` is tar-stream''s \"size mismatch\" path: a file in a template\nchanged size between `lstat` and reading it. That destroys the pack,\nwhich rejects the write `pipeline` in `packDirectory`. `packDirectory`\nonly awaits that pipeline after the last entry, so the rejection is\nunhandled and Node exits. One template''s failure takes down the whole\nscript instead of being reported as that template''s failure.\n\nThree more problems sit behind that:\n\n- A failed pack leaves a truncated tarball at the destination. Once the\ncrash is fixed, e2e would extract a broken template instead of falling\nback.\n- The error doesn''t say which file was changing, so the actual writer\ncan''t be identified.\n- By design, the task only failed when *every* template failed. But its\noutput is cached, so a run with one template missing was cached as a\nsuccess, and that package manager and preset stayed on the slow\n`create-nx-workspace` fallback on every later cache hit.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\n`packDirectory` handles the pipeline rejection as soon as it creates it,\nso a failed pack rejects only that call. On failure it also:\n\n- deletes the partial tarball, so e2e takes the existing\n`create-nx-workspace` fallback;\n- names the file in the error, with its size at `lstat` and afterwards\n(e.g. `Could not pack <path> (0 bytes at lstat, 400000 now): size\nmismatch`).\n\n`populate-e2e-base-workspace` now fails when **any** template fails,\nwith the usual `Failed to build the <pm>/<preset> base workspace: …`\nreport, so an incomplete set is never cached.\n\nThis change doesn''t guess at which file was being written in CI. The\nnext failure will name it, and that can be excluded or waited on with\nevidence.\n\nVerified with a local repro that packs a directory while one file keeps\ngrowing:\n\n- Before: `allSettled` records the rejection, then the process still\ndies with `ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE` (exit 1), and a 13.8 MB partial\ntarball is left behind.\n- After: the template''s rejection names the file and is reported instead\nof crashing the process, and no tarball is left behind.\n- A pack/extract round trip of files and symlinks is unchanged.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nN/A\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/Run-rspack-and-rsbuild-unit-tests-with-vitest-4a161f55\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->","tree":{"sha":"cc6f8ce3cffcd32577f68ddddf6a3be1fb2ea8de","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/trees/cc6f8ce3cffcd32577f68ddddf6a3be1fb2ea8de"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/commits/30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689","verification":{"payload":"tree cc6f8ce3cffcd32577f68ddddf6a3be1fb2ea8de\nparent 8e5b27731b9a7f9587a5643e807b4d259be3a41c\nauthor Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com> 1790357731 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790357731 -0400\n\nfix(repo): make e2e base workspace template failures clean and uncached (#37194)\n\n## Current Behavior\n\n`populate-e2e-base-workspace`\n(`scripts/local-registry/build-e2e-base-workspace.mjs`) can crash\noutright, as it just did on `main-macos` for #37173:\n\n```\nWrote base workspace template: .../proj-backup/bun-apps.tar\nWrote base workspace template: .../proj-backup/bun-ts.tar\nnode:internal/process/promises:324\n    triggerUncaughtException(err, true /* fromPromise */);\nError [ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE]: Premature close\n    at Pack.destroy (tar-stream/pack.js:203:8)\n    at Sink.<anonymous> (tar-stream/pack.js:174:12)\n```\n\n`pack.js:174` is tar-stream''s \"size mismatch\" path: a file in a template\nchanged size between `lstat` and reading it. That destroys the pack,\nwhich rejects the write `pipeline` in `packDirectory`. `packDirectory`\nonly awaits that pipeline after the last entry, so the rejection is\nunhandled and Node exits. One template''s failure takes down the whole\nscript instead of being reported as that template''s failure.\n\nThree more problems sit behind that:\n\n- A failed pack leaves a truncated tarball at the destination. Once the\ncrash is fixed, e2e would extract a broken template instead of falling\nback.\n- The error doesn''t say which file was changing, so the actual writer\ncan''t be identified.\n- By design, the task only failed when *every* template failed. But its\noutput is cached, so a run with one template missing was cached as a\nsuccess, and that package manager and preset stayed on the slow\n`create-nx-workspace` fallback on every later cache hit.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\n`packDirectory` handles the pipeline rejection as soon as it creates it,\nso a failed pack rejects only that call. On failure it also:\n\n- deletes the partial tarball, so e2e takes the existing\n`create-nx-workspace` fallback;\n- names the file in the error, with its size at `lstat` and afterwards\n(e.g. `Could not pack <path> (0 bytes at lstat, 400000 now): size\nmismatch`).\n\n`populate-e2e-base-workspace` now fails when **any** template fails,\nwith the usual `Failed to build the <pm>/<preset> base workspace: …`\nreport, so an incomplete set is never cached.\n\nThis change doesn''t guess at which file was being written in CI. The\nnext failure will name it, and that can be excluded or waited on with\nevidence.\n\nVerified with a local repro that packs a directory while one file keeps\ngrowing:\n\n- Before: `allSettled` records the rejection, then the process still\ndies with `ERR_STREAM_PREMATURE_CLOSE` (exit 1), and a 13.8 MB partial\ntarball is left behind.\n- After: the template''s rejection names the file and is reported instead\nof crashing the process, and no tarball is left behind.\n- A pack/extract round trip of files and symlinks is unchanged.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nN/A\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/Run-rspack-and-rsbuild-unit-tests-with-vitest-4a161f55\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtrDjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAgFkQAF4R/ODW2H+ATLGdijNA2wHn\ng3UF7py2KOa7yytafcc84TKNalxyKPhO15yd66btCDANbFiHFAXleOZBEpq5jD/Z\nk5xyXcBuAv+RGb9sQGPqz4gEvPooZOXK13ZE/+hC6z9N9TgL3mth+Nn71n8+AY9O\nPQOIkK+nEuXn1qqhyYHFI8ozcAwDpsjNgM+Ymx3Rq+fIQY8ngkTfYw5k+x5rOJAT\nBRtjrekoyWSHmG6WnBh/bfsiOUegsAzQhNcJcoZcYoBuL2+fYwvn/CVeVI7yxZXd\nIY22OjQR7w4eofeXMNRerMqGezf4QSDyOikMMzkoSltF9F9XuH2i7YD2lanp5+M4\nlXRkVXBe767/5TjbHaTBIHIMREdJaOZu2p7GHGdWZC9ewkMniAt6YTXnEvjc3+sA\nTFGH+ybziI8YXXCKPNErKveQJjexHvjBMjGRZNLwu0BTdTxo1jqGqJ5hrOdIzPad\nE4+ZhBiIiby4krzTb3FcSLqWw9d0q1Sv61M1Jc3AIvid9LZhKtGoI+1Y/JBBshTl\n49c8zhyQhHPvrDnK5bhbaBs6FjjnOrNsmrNZkYn6rQkWBE7Zghua4B+CFf3+0qRJ\nf2S4NjLo2UlLGeDFgbn2PA+MVJemMmdnwR7EW18f3c++NSm5ifsX6rCxLXBw9J/Z\n5bGjYuBUiJ8KQmmS1r2T\n=xunU\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-25T17:35:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689","node_id":"C_kwDOBfbSFNoAKDMwYTgxYmIwMDcyOTRlNWRhMTVmNDllOGVlNTJlYjA2NGYwNDg2ODk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/nrwl/nx/commit/8e5b27731b9a7f9587a5643e807b4d259be3a41c","sha":"8e5b27731b9a7f9587a5643e807b4d259be3a41c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/8e5b27731b9a7f9587a5643e807b4d259be3a41c"}],"sha":"30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/30a81bb007294e5da15f49e8ee52eb064f048689"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/8104246?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/FrozenPandaz","id":8104246,"login":"FrozenPandaz","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjgxMDQyNDY=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/FrozenPandaz","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/commits/8e5b27731b9a7f9587a5643e807b4d259be3a41c/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-25T17:08:31Z","email":"jasonjean1993@gmail.com","name":"Jason Jean"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-25T17:08:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(core): replace the sandbox target property with ultracache (#37165)\n\nStacked on #37036. Review the last commit only.\n\n## Current Behavior\n\nA target configures observed IO through `sandbox`, with two independent\nbooleans:\n\n```jsonc\n{ \"sandbox\": { \"enabled\": false, \"backfill\": false, \"ignoredReads\": [], \"ignoredWrites\": [] } }\n```\n\n`enabled` governs whether IO is recorded; `backfill` governs whether a\nrecording may stand in for the target''s declared inputs and outputs. The\npair can express states nobody wants — recording off with backfill on —\nand neither name says what the feature is for.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe property is `ultracache`, and the two booleans collapse into one\n`mode`:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"ultracache\": {\n    \"mode\": \"warn\",\n    \"ignoredReads\": [\"tmp/cache/**\"],\n    \"ignoredWrites\": [\"scratch/**\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Mode | Records IO | Reports undeclared access | Applies the recording\n|\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `on` (default) | yes | no | yes |\n| `warn` | yes | warns | no |\n| `error` | yes | fails the task | no |\n| `off` | no | no | no |\n\n- `enabled: false` becomes `mode: \"off\"`; `backfill: false` becomes\n`mode: \"warn\"`.\n- `mode` is a real union rather than a string. `UltracacheMode` is\n`#[napi(string_enum = \"lowercase\")]`, so the generated bindings carry\n`''on'' | ''warn'' | ''error'' | ''off''` and the values stay lowercase.\n- One axis rather than two also removes the combination that made a\nrecording ambiguous: there is no way to record reads without writes, so\nan entry can never arrive looking like \"this task read nothing\".\n- The snapshot eligibility walk matches on the enum; the fallback reason\n`backfill-disabled` becomes `autofix-disabled`, covering both `warn` and\n`error`.\n- Nx Cloud only. Nothing in the OSS runner records or applies IO, so\nevery mode behaves as `off` without it, and the docs say so.\n\n## No migration\n\n`sandbox` has only reached `23.3.0-canary.20260924`; `23.3.0-beta.5`\npredates it. Renaming is free until a beta ships the old name.\n\n## Cloud side\n\nnrwl/ocean reads `task.sandbox` in merged code (client bundle, the Go\nio-trace daemon, and the Kotlin api). A matching rename is needed there,\nand must land first so the readers are ready.\n\n## Verification\n\n`nx test nx` fully green — 372 files, 8275 specs, 758 Rust tests. `tsc\n--noEmit` on the spec project, `nx lint nx`, vale and\n`check-wasm-target.sh` all clean.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nFollow-up to #36853 (NXC-4922), stacked on #37036.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01AsRdDAvgjR2kDk1qtUjMCd\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/alert-puma-61c23833\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"baabee8fb3494735407ca826038f67ba067ed3b0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/trees/baabee8fb3494735407ca826038f67ba067ed3b0"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/nrwl/nx/git/commits/8e5b27731b9a7f9587a5643e807b4d259be3a41c","verification":{"payload":"tree baabee8fb3494735407ca826038f67ba067ed3b0\nparent 2cfeb623a8cdc1dab6064fbddaa41293588ac340\nauthor Jason Jean <jasonjean1993@gmail.com> 1790356111 -0400\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790356111 +0000\n\nfeat(core): replace the sandbox target property with ultracache (#37165)\n\nStacked on #37036. Review the last commit only.\n\n## Current Behavior\n\nA target configures observed IO through `sandbox`, with two independent\nbooleans:\n\n```jsonc\n{ \"sandbox\": { \"enabled\": false, \"backfill\": false, \"ignoredReads\": [], \"ignoredWrites\": [] } }\n```\n\n`enabled` governs whether IO is recorded; `backfill` governs whether a\nrecording may stand in for the target''s declared inputs and outputs. The\npair can express states nobody wants — recording off with backfill on —\nand neither name says what the feature is for.\n\n## Expected Behavior\n\nThe property is `ultracache`, and the two booleans collapse into one\n`mode`:\n\n```jsonc\n{\n  \"ultracache\": {\n    \"mode\": \"warn\",\n    \"ignoredReads\": [\"tmp/cache/**\"],\n    \"ignoredWrites\": [\"scratch/**\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n| Mode | Records IO | Reports undeclared access | Applies the recording\n|\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `on` (default) | yes | no | yes |\n| `warn` | yes | warns | no |\n| `error` | yes | fails the task | no |\n| `off` | no | no | no |\n\n- `enabled: false` becomes `mode: \"off\"`; `backfill: false` becomes\n`mode: \"warn\"`.\n- `mode` is a real union rather than a string. `UltracacheMode` is\n`#[napi(string_enum = \"lowercase\")]`, so the generated bindings carry\n`''on'' | ''warn'' | ''error'' | ''off''` and the values stay lowercase.\n- One axis rather than two also removes the combination that made a\nrecording ambiguous: there is no way to record reads without writes, so\nan entry can never arrive looking like \"this task read nothing\".\n- The snapshot eligibility walk matches on the enum; the fallback reason\n`backfill-disabled` becomes `autofix-disabled`, covering both `warn` and\n`error`.\n- Nx Cloud only. Nothing in the OSS runner records or applies IO, so\nevery mode behaves as `off` without it, and the docs say so.\n\n## No migration\n\n`sandbox` has only reached `23.3.0-canary.20260924`; `23.3.0-beta.5`\npredates it. Renaming is free until a beta ships the old name.\n\n## Cloud side\n\nnrwl/ocean reads `task.sandbox` in merged code (client bundle, the Go\nio-trace daemon, and the Kotlin api). A matching rename is needed there,\nand must land first so the readers are ready.\n\n## Verification\n\n`nx test nx` fully green — 372 files, 8275 specs, 758 Rust tests. `tsc\n--noEmit` on the spec project, `nx lint nx`, vale and\n`check-wasm-target.sh` all clean.\n\n## Related Issue(s)\n\nFollow-up to #36853 (NXC-4922), stacked on #37036.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01AsRdDAvgjR2kDk1qtUjMCd\n\n<!-- polygraph-session-start -->\n---\n<p><a\nhref=\"https://app.trypolygraph.com/orgs/6a061dcb561c062131116eca/sessions/alert-puma-61c23833\">View\nPolygraph session ↗</a></p>\n<!-- polygraph-session-end -->\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqtqqPCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA1yYQABG07O42wVNmZWoJUXL3aoe5\nOCkctwj1QvJY3KRHb7WbWb+H8KMR2cmB0PQ7N2j21oX4hNBw5KYSQYtYFcw/qLEj\nuU26EGHM0jNccYmEe8t7NZpwSeEkeMAMsWvW6xRuSRhPqpTmzFh/Yr6rIXX/k6+g\n0GGIHm2L3xpjHzYI1rEFab7lIS3q4LJrLxCp6ix7SFs5ymV4kg6RUJ